  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing a Preloader**\n\nOkay, I\'m working on a minimalist preloader for the page. It\'ll use the brand colors, specifically Cream, Dark Graphite, and Gold. I\'m focusing on making it appear during the initial load, which should improve how fast the page feels. The React app with GSAP is the main environment to consider.\n\n\n**Implementing the Preloader**\n\nI\'ve decided to build a `Preloader` component and placed it in `/src/components`. It\'s a full-screen, minimalist design using brand colors, and includes a simple loading animation. I\'m using Framer Motion for the exit animation, making it slide away smoothly. I\'ve set up a simulated loading state using `useEffect` with a timeout for the initial test run.\n\n\n**Refining the Animation**\n\nI\'ve been tweaking the animation details. Currently, I am working on the loading indicator; I like the pulsing circle. I added a text element for \"Favo\" that slides up, and a subtle line that animates across the bottom. I\'m using `motion.div` from Framer Motion to control the animations. I think it looks very clean.\n\n\n**Integrating the Preloader**\n\nOkay, I\'m integrating the `Preloader` component. I realized there was a dependency confusion regarding Framer Motion - I needed to check where the Framer Motion library was imported from to ensure the correct path. It\'s now being included in the root component, `App.tsx`, and I\'ve ensured it\'s rendering there, wrapping the application.\n\n\n**Addressing Animation Timing**\n\nI\'m now focusing on animation timing and how the preloader interacts with scroll-triggered GSAP animations. I\'m considering potential issues with animations firing prematurely if a user scrolls while content is hidden by the preloader. I\'ve realized the optimal approach is to either delay GSAP animations or to ensure the preloader remains active until animations are ready. A `Layout.tsx` component might be useful for managing scroll functionality in conjunction with the preloader, perhaps using `Lenis`. I\'m leaning toward keeping things simple and implementing the preloader in `App.tsx` for ease of management.\n\n\n**Adjusting Route Transitions**\n\nI\'m now dealing with a problem: the preloader doesn\'t re-appear on route changes, which is good, but the content\'s GSAP animations begin before the user sees the content, if they navigate to the page after the initial load. To fix this, I have to ensure the routes, and their associated animations, don\'t trigger until the preloader is finished. I\'m considering using global state or delaying the rendering of `<Layout>` until the `isLoading` state is `false`.\n\n\n**Refining the Overlay**\n\nI\'m revisiting the implementation to ensure a smooth transition between the preloader and the content. I realize that using `{!isLoading && <Layout>...` prevents the exit animation from overlapping. I\'m now thinking of the preloader as an absolute overlay, to achieve the desired effect of the page animations occurring *after* the preloader has fully animated offscreen.\n\n\n**Optimizing Animation Triggers**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how to synchronize the GSAP animations with the preloader, especially across different routes. I was initially looking at `delay` in GSAP and potential global states, but the approach with `sessionStorage` to track first visits is interesting. It allows to simply delay the home animation for a specific time. I\'m now testing various delays to see how the preloader and content animations interact. I\'m considering the best way to handle route transitions after the initial load, using a simple but effective strategy.\n\n\n**Refining Loading Logic**\n\nI\'m now refining the loading logic to ensure the preloader disappears smoothly. I\'ve focused on `useEffect` to manage the loading state, and I\'m ensuring `document.body.style.overflow` is reset. The animation is working well, but I\'m testing the timeout durations. I\'m making sure to manage any side effects cleanly, and I\'m considering all exit conditions.\n\n\n**Finalizing the Indicator**\n\nI\'m now very happy with the visual indicator, it has a pulsing circle, the \"Favo\" text that slides up, and a subtle line that animates across the bottom. The implementation is based on Framer Motion\'s `motion.div`. I feel this component is good for production.\n\n\n**Synchronizing Route Logic**\n\nI\'m now integrating logic for route changes using `sessionStorage`. My plan is to synchronize the Hero animation in `Home.tsx` and other route animations, ensuring they start after the preloader finishes on initial load, but immediately on subsequent navigations. I am now working on ensuring content animations are correctly delayed after the initial load, using the `sessionStorage` value to determine the delay. I\'m now testing and refining the animation.\n\n\n**Implementing Route Delays**\n\nI\'m now integrating route delays using `sessionStorage`. My approach involves setting a flag after the initial preloader animation. I am now working on ensuring content animations are correctly delayed after the initial load, using the `sessionStorage` value to manage initial delays. I\'m testing the animations and their timing across multiple routes.\n\n\n**Refining Preloader Logic**\n\nI\'m now refining the preloader\'s loading state management, focusing on using `sessionStorage` to track visits. I\'m leveraging `useEffect` to manage the preloader\'s display and exit conditions, handling the initial load and subsequent route changes efficiently. I\'m focusing on ensuring seamless transitions and preventing any content flashing.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 33
      nanos: 28014529
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
