  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining the Scope**\n\nI\'m zeroing in on the testimonial component\'s core function: showcasing client wins and addressing financial concerns. The user\'s request is for a carousel interface with GSAP animation for fluid movement, and I\'m ensuring it aligns with existing design elements.\n\n\n**Assessing Design Elements**\n\nI\'ve been analyzing the current design system. I\'m focusing on the elegant, minimalist aesthetic, paying close attention to color palettes like the cream background, card background and muted text. I\'m noting the font choices: Cormorant Garamond and Montserrat, along with their usage and styling. I\'m also examining the thin borders. This is critical for integrating the testimonial component seamlessly.\n\n\n**Implementing the Animation**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the animation implementation with React and GSAP. I\'ve considered potential conflicts and determined that using a state index to control opacity and x-position of a container `ref` provides a solid approach for slide and fade transitions upon index changes. I\'m leaning toward this solution for a fluid user experience.\n\n\n**Refining the Animation Logic**\n\nI\'ve streamlined the GSAP implementation. Initially, I kept all slides in the DOM and animated their positions, but I\'ve now adopted a simpler approach, using React state and the `useGSAP` hook for a more manageable flow. The `currentIndex` state controls slide visibility and animation triggers, resulting in a cleaner and more efficient system. I\'m focusing on ensuring smooth transitions between testimonials.\n\n\n**Optimizing the Transitions**\n\nI\'ve significantly refined the animation implementation further. I\'ve moved towards a \"track\" approach using `display: flex` and `transform: translateX()`, which GSAP animates for a buttery smooth slide. The `currentIndex` still drives the transitions, now affecting the `xPercent` of the track. This is far cleaner and more standard than my previous approach.\n\n\n\n**Positioning the Component**\n\nMy thoughts have evolved to encompass placement. I\'ve decided the testimonial section best fits after the \"Como Posso Ajudar\" section, as social proof naturally follows the pitch. I am now in the midst of outlining the technical implementation. I\'m focusing on defining the component structure and its placement within the page.\n\n\n**Implementing the Controls**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the control implementation for the carousel. Considering the design system, I\'m leaning toward minimalist arrow buttons, positioned at the bottom right or center. I\'ll need to import the relevant icons from `lucide-react`, including `ChevronLeft`, `ChevronRight`, and `Quote`. I\'ll also be integrating the necessary React hooks and GSAP. My focus is on ensuring these controls are intuitive and aesthetically aligned.\n\n\n**Finalizing the Component**\n\nI\'ve just finalized the component\'s structure and the approach. I\'ve built the track layout using `display: flex; flex-wrap: nowrap;`, which allows for seamless horizontal scrolling. I\'m now integrating the testimonials data, ensuring they\'re correctly displayed within the carousel. The next step is a final review, ensuring all constraints are met.\n\n\n**Refining the Controls**\n\nI\'ve just implemented the control buttons, using minimalist arrow icons from `lucide-react`, and styled them to match the design system. I\'ve integrated `prev` and `next` functions for intuitive carousel navigation. I\'m leveraging `gsap.to()` and `xPercent` to smoothly translate the track, transitioning between slides based on the `currentIndex`. The `power4.inOut` easing provides a more refined, professional touch.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 33
      nanos: 24441694
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
