  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining the Solution Framework**\n\nI\'m now focusing on structuring the core comparison framework, specifically the \"Pain Points vs. Favored Solutions\" grid. My aim is to make it visually clear and pedagogically sound for financial corporate struggles, ensuring it\'s not just informative, but also educational and very clean in its presentation.\n\n\n**Refining the Target Audience**\n\nI\'ve clarified the target audience: service providers, like dentists and architects, who grapple with financial disorganization. The objective now is to cultivate a reassuring, empathetic tone while maintaining clarity. I\'m focusing on the visual design, ensuring it\'s both clean and aligns with the color palette. I\'m also planning to use animations and icons for added emphasis. I\'m starting the comparison format, specifically problem/solution for two items.\n\n\n**Expanding the Core Comparisons**\n\nI\'m now expanding the comparison grid with more specific pain points and corresponding solutions. I\'ve added a few new items focusing on time constraints, financial blindness, and pricing issues. I\342\200\231m thinking through how to visually represent these points effectively within the framework, using a table-like grid, possibly with distinct color coding or faded text for the \"pain\" side. I\'m aiming for concise yet impactful presentations.\n\n\n**Visualizing the Layout**\n\nI\'m now implementing the visual layout details. I\'ve decided on a row-based structure, comparing \"Como \303\251 hoje\" to \"Com a Favo\" (How it is today vs. With Favo). The \"pain\" side will have a light grey background or a subtle \"X\" icon, while the \"solution\" side will be cream/white with a gold checkmark. A stacked card or side-by-side flex layout for each item looks to be the most readable approach.\n\n**Coding the Interactive Elements**\n\nI\'m now writing the code. I\'m importing React, Lucide icons, and GSAP, and I\'m setting up ScrollTrigger animations. I will be applying Tailwind styling using the theme colors exactly. I\'ve designed the background for the section as white or cream. I am also working on the row styling, pain and solution colors.\n\n**Improving the Design Language**\n\nI\'m focusing on refining the copy and aesthetics, aiming for a minimalist design that avoids overly complex elements. I plan to incorporate elegant typography, using a font-serif for titles. The goal is a clean, modern aesthetic.\n\n\n**Implementing the Component**\n\nI\'m now implementing the component, starting with a lightweight, accessible approach, using `font-light` for the body and staggered fade-in animations on scroll. I\'m structuring the comparison data using TypeScript with an array of objects. I need to make sure the target audience is clear: service providers with professional financial struggles. The component will be a `ComparisonGrid.tsx` in `Home.tsx` right after the Trust Indicators. I\'m starting to draft the code, importing `X`, and `Check`.\n\n\n**Adjusting the Messaging**\n\nI\'m now refining the text for a clearer tone. I\'ve rephrased the pain points and solutions. I\'m focusing on simplicity and clarity, using easily understandable language, like \"Sal\303\241rio claro e previs\303\255vel\" instead of jargon. I am also making them more direct and relatable, ensuring an empathetic approach. I am focusing on pain points and solutions for the finance component.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 25
      nanos: 855544388
    }
