  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining the Scope**\n\nI\'m currently focused on defining the scope. The goal is clear: implement a \"magnetic hover\" effect on the CTA buttons using GSAP. I\'m considering this a common premium web design pattern and am starting to break it down.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Button Targets**\n\nI\'ve identified the primary CTA within `Home.tsx`, specifically the \"Agendar Diagn\303\263stico\" button in the hero section, as the initial target. Further, I\'m now examining the `Navbar/Header` to check for other potential CTAs to apply the effect to. I\'m leaning toward creating a reusable `MagneticButton` component, to be able to use it widely. The plan is to develop this `MagneticButton` component (`MagneticButton.tsx`) which will wrap children, to make it easy to manage. It seems I will have to manage both the react-router-dom Link and standard anchor tags.\n\n\n**Constructing the Wrapper**\n\nI\'m now drafting the `MagneticWrapper` component. This will house the GSAP listeners for the magnetic effect, attaching them to its child element. My plan is to make the effect customizable, with a `strength` prop to control the intensity. The logic includes listening for `mousemove` to calculate mouse distance from the button center and moving it appropriately. On `mouseleave`, the button will smoothly return to its original position. I\'m using `useGSAP` from `@gsap/react` for lifecycle management.\n\n\n**Refining the Logic**\n\nI\'ve just added the mouse event listeners within `MagneticWrapper`. I\'m using `gsap.quickTo` for efficient animation to both X and Y. I\'m focusing on the `mousemove` event, calculating the distance between the mouse and the button\'s center, scaling the X and Y movement based on the `strength` prop. Also, I\'ve got `mouseleave` configured to smooth the element back to its initial position. The cleanup function is ready.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 8
      nanos: 822711576
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
