  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining the Requirement**\n\nOkay, I\'m working on defining the core requirement here. The user needs a \"Reveal Effect\" image slider. It sounds like the key is contrasting a messy spreadsheet (Before) with a cleaned-up version (After) for financial visuals. I\'m focusing on that core contrast right now.\n\n\n**Locating the Component**\n\nI\'ve determined I\'ll create `BeforeAfterReveal.tsx` as a standalone section in `Home.tsx`. The goal is to visually demonstrate the before-and-after, contrasting a messy spreadsheet with Favo\'s clean dashboard. I\'m thinking of placing it right above or below the `ComparisonGrid`, or possibly before the `FounderLetter` section. I\'ve narrowed it down to the Comparison Grid, just after or before.\n\n\n**Implementing the Drag**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the slider interaction. I\'ll use a range input hidden over the images for accessibility and easy mobile support. This approach, using an input with opacity 0, will control the width/clip-path of the top \"before\" image. I\'ve selected Unsplash images. For \"before,\" I\'m considering https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1554224155-8d04cb21cd6c?auto=format&fit=crop&q=80&w=1200 or https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1581291518633-83b4ebd1d83e?auto=format&fit=crop&q=80&w=1200, and for \"after,\" https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1551288049-bebda4e38f71?auto=format&fit=crop&q=80&w=1200.\n\n\n**Refining the Implementation**\n\nI\'ve decided using a `clip-path` property is the most direct route for the reveal effect. I\'m imagining how a range input, invisible, will control the width of the \"before\" image, revealing the \"after\" image underneath. I\'m exploring the structure, and it looks like it will have the before image as a background element with the after image being the foreground, and then clipped.\n\n\n**Optimizing the Presentation**\n\nI\'m now integrating the slider handle as a visual cue. I\'m placing an invisible range input, that will drive a visual handle. This invisible input controls the \"before\" image\'s width, and the corresponding visual handle will show the state of the before-and-after with an arrow icon. I am still deciding how I want to show the \'Before\' and \'After\' states.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 21
      nanos: 410163836
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
