  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Refining the Target**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the \"Antes e Depois\" section, specifically the `BeforeAfterReveal.tsx` component. The key issue is its lack of personality; it comes across as sterile and AI-generated, which needs immediate attention.\n\n\n**Developing Animation Concepts**\n\nI\'ve been exploring animation concepts to inject personality into the Before/After slider. I\'m focusing on connected and interactive effects. So far, I\'ve got two promising ideas. The first, dynamic, floating badges that correlate with the slider position. The second concept involves dynamic typography, where the title morphs based on the slider percentage, which will make an impression on the user. I\'m aiming for something beyond basic fades, encouraging both attention and interaction.\n\n\n**Implementing Interactive Details**\n\nI\'m now integrating Framer Motion to create a springy, magnetic slider handle that expands and glows on hover. The goal is elegant interactivity. I\'m also adding floating, animated data points linked to the slider\'s position, providing context. \"Caos\" side shows negative financial indicators, and \"Clareza\" side will display positive ones. The design is intended to have personality and encourage user engagement.\n\n\n**Adding Dynamic Widgets**\n\nI\'m now focusing on interactive widgets within the reveal component. I\'m exploring mouse-tracking on the container for the slider, making it a fully interactive and engaging experience. The \"Caos\" side will have a reddish overlay with a potential shake effect. I plan to add floating UI elements to add personality, like a crumpled invoice, on the \"Caos\" side and something sleek on the \"Clareza\" side.\n\n\n**Designing Interactive Widgets**\n\nI\'m now drafting the code for `BeforeAfterReveal.tsx` to implement those interactive widgets, using `framer-motion` for spring interpolation of the slider position with `useMotionValue` and `useSpring`. The goal is parallax on floating elements. \"Caos\" will include a \"Boleto Vencido\" warning, a \"Saldo Negativo\" note, and \"Cad\303\252 a nota?\" from the accountant. \"Clareza\" will showcase \"Previs\303\243o de Caixa: Positiva\", \"Lucro L\303\255quido: 24%\" with a chart or glowing approval badge, and \"Tudo em dia!\". I need this animation to grab the user\'s attention.\n\n\n**Constructing The Interaction**\n\nI\'m now building a \"Smart Reveal\" component that goes beyond a static before/after display. The plan is to make the entire container interactive, not just the slider. My focus will be on `onPointerMove` with Framer Motion for smooth, spring-like slider movement. I\'m aiming for dynamic heading text and glassmorphism cards that appear as the slider moves, adding a subtle parallax effect to the images.\n\n\n**Implementing User Interaction**\n\nI\'m now focusing on interactive widgets within the reveal component. I\'ve integrated `getBoundingClientRect()` to precisely track the mouse position. I\'m using this information to calculate the relative X coordinate (`relX`) and percentage (`pct`) to accurately control the slider. The goal is a highly precise slider control for a seamless experience. The \"Caos\" side will have a red/dark aesthetic, with `AlertTriangle` and `FileText`. \"Clareza\" will be gold/light, featuring `CheckCircle2` and `TrendingUp`. The slider itself will be a premium glassmorphism pill with arrows.\n\n\n**Rewriting the Component**\n\nI\'m now implementing the rewritten `BeforeAfterReveal.tsx` component. I will use the `default_api:run_command` to insert the new code into the file and trigger a build/lint. I also fixed the `motion` import and ensured that the spring is correctly mapped to `clipPath` using `useTransform`.\n\n\n**Clarifying Image Clipping**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the clipping logic within `BeforeAfterReveal.tsx`. It seems `clipPathBefore` is actually handling the *After* image clipping, not the *Before*, which is the expected behavior. I need to be absolutely certain of this order as I integrate other components.\n\n\n**Defining the Clipping Logic**\n\nI\'m now nailing down the clipping strategy. I\'ve realized `Before` is the foreground, clipped from the *right*, and `After` is the background, not clipped. A 20% slider means 20% \"Caos\" on the left. The `clipPath` for \"Caos\" is `inset(0 (100 - slider)% 0 0)`. The existing plan is to animate floating cards based on the slider, and the clip path means they will *also* be \"wiped\" neatly.\n\n\n**Constructing Card Overlays**\n\nI\'m now building the HTML structure for the slider component. I\'m focusing on the layered approach, where the \"After\" image and a \"Tudo em dia\" card is the base layer. On top, the \"Before\" image and a \"Boleto Vencido\" card will be clipped with a spring-like animation. The slider handle is added for interactive control.\n\n\n\n**Building the Interface**\n\nI\'m now focusing on integrating the proposed card overlays with the slider component. I\'m leveraging Framer Motion for the floating animations on both \"Caos\" and \"Clareza\" sides. The `motion.div` structure is in place for a dynamic feel, using a looping vertical movement to create the pulsating effect. I\'m also preparing the magnetic slider handle interaction.\n\n\n**Refining Interactivity**\n\nI\'m now integrating mouse tracking across the entire container for slider control, aiming for fluid interaction. I\'m focusing on `onPointerMove` and `onPointerLeave` for a responsive feel. I\'m using `useTransform` with `springXPct` to correctly control the `clipPath`. Building the `BeforeAfterReveal.tsx` component is my priority now, so that users can enjoy the real-time slider updates.\n\n\n**Finalizing Clip Path**\n\nI\'m now putting the finishing touches on the clip path and related animations, ensuring the before and after states transition smoothly. I am setting the \"Caos\" and \"Clareza\" cards now, ensuring the slider handles the interactive components effectively. My focus is on robustly integrating dynamic content updates for an engaging user experience.\n\n\n\n**Implementing Card Placement**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the precise positioning of the overlay cards, ensuring they don\'t obscure the slider handle at the 50% mark. I\'ve defined the card placements on both sides to avoid overlap and align them so that they resemble SaaS elements. I\'m adding specific styling for each card: distinct backgrounds, text colors, and subtle rotations for visual appeal.\n\n\n**Adding Parallax Details**\n\nI\'m now integrating subtle parallax effects into the background images of the slider. I\'ll slightly scale and move them horizontally in relation to the slider position to create a sense of depth. Since the `clipPath` cuts the container, the images inside can fill it, but be transformed. The slice edge will add movement, and it makes the entire experience feel more premium.\n\n\n"
