  }
  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining the Visual Goal**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the visual aspect of the animation. The core idea is to smoothly connect \"pain points\" to \"solution points\" through an interactive SVG component controlled by scroll. The visual needs to clearly represent this transition as the user scrolls, maybe some sort of line animation?\n\n\n\n**Refining the Animation**\n\nI\'m now thinking about the specific animation style. I want an organic, hand-drawn look for the connecting line, moving away from anything that feels generic. I\'m planning to build this as a React component, leveraging GSAP and its `ScrollTrigger` plugin. The plan is to place this section on the home page, either as a standalone piece or to bridge existing content.\n\n\n\n**Developing the Visual Path**\n\nI\'m now making solid progress on the SVG path animation itself. I\'m focusing on making a curvy path, scrubbing its `stroke-dasharray` from full length to zero based on scroll. I\'m also planning to animate the visibility or scale of each node on the path as the line reaches them, indicating progress along this \"journey\". I\'m thinking of titling this section \"A Jornada Favo\" to better frame the user experience.\n\n\n**Constructing the Visual Story**\n\nI\'m now diving into the SVG\'s construction, opting for a custom timeline approach. The structure will be a relative container with an absolute SVG. The path itself will be golden. I\'m exploring a left-to-right \"Caos\" to \"Clareza\" transition, mapping pain points to solution points with a GSAP-powered golden line. I am planning on working with the viewBox and explicit layout to make the SVG responsive.\n\n\n**Simplifying the Implementation**\n\nI\'m now moving toward a more straightforward implementation. I\'ve decided to use HTML elements for the nodes in the timeline and a simplified SVG path. A vertical line with a slight weave and `vector-effect=\"non-scaling-stroke\"` will serve to connect the dots. I\'m also planning to leverage the GSAP DrawSVG alternative to animate the path.\n\n\n**Implementing the Narrative**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the specific stages: \"A Metodologia Favo\" and \"Previsibilidade.\" The desktop layout will alternate the visual placement left and right, with a snaking SVG line connecting them, while mobile will place everything on the left. I\'m executing the code in `JourneyTimeline.tsx`, and planning to integrate it before `BeforeAfterReveal` on `Home.tsx` to set the stage for the hook. I\'ll use `motion/react` if needed and keep the SVG organic.\n\n\n**Refining the Layout**\n\nI\'m now iterating on the layout strategy. Using `preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"` introduces distortion, so I\'ve pivoted. I\'ll center a fixed-width SVG (e.g., 1200px) and absolutely position HTML elements over specific SVG coordinates for pixel-perfect alignment. I have mapped the nodes and crafted the path using Bezier curves: chaos to break to order to peace and profit. I\'m taking the desktop view into account.\n\n\n**Adapting the Implementation**\n\nI\'m now shifting gears on the visual approach, opting to use HTML nodes overlaid with a dynamic SVG path, dynamically updated on DOM changes. I\'ll get X,Y coordinates of HTML nodes via javascript, construct the SVG\'s `d` attribute string and animate it with GSAP. It\'s time to refine the central winding line and CSS flexbox for content display.\n\n\n**Designing the Vertical Timeline**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the architecture of the journey component, and I\'m pivoting to a vertical timeline approach that will work consistently across both mobile and desktop. I\'m imagining a central, curving SVG path running down the middle, visually connecting the stages. The component will have a container with two columns, but for now I\'m working on a set of four steps. The stages are: *O Caos*, *A Sobrecarga*, *A Virada*, and *O Controle*.\n\n\n**Refining the Animation**\n\nI\'m now implementing the SVG path animation, registering GSAP\'s ScrollTrigger plugin and calculating the path\'s total length for `strokeDasharray`. I\'ve set up a GSAP timeline to scrub the `strokeDashoffset` from full length to zero based on scroll progress within the container, giving life to the central curve. I\'m also preparing to animate individual dots along the journey.\n\n\n\n**Implementing Dot Animation**\n\nI\'m now focusing on animating the individual dots and journey cards to synchronize with the path animation. Using GSAP\'s `scrollTrigger`, I\'m orchestrating animations for each dot to scale and fade in when they come into view. Simultaneously, I\'m animating the corresponding content cards, making them slide up and fade in at their respective scroll triggers. My aim is to create a harmonious and engaging visual flow.\n\n\n**Synchronizing the Elements**\n\nI\'m now synchronizing the dot and card animations with the SVG path\'s progress. I\'m using `scrollTrigger` to ensure each element scales and fades in precisely as the animated path reaches its corresponding point. I\'ve updated both the visibility and the layout of the UI to make these changes visually accessible.\n\n\n**Refining the Path\'s Structure**\n\nI\'m now thinking through the path structure in more detail. A straightforward path with curves is appealing, and I\'ll map HTML elements to the SVG nodes. For the desktop view, I\'m envisioning a classic zig-zag timeline: Pain 1 on the left, Path in the center curving right, then an empty space on the right. This arrangement continues, alternating positions for pain points and solution points, connected by the central path. I\'ll focus on building `OrganicJourney.tsx` next.\n\n\n**Mapping the Element Placement**\n\nI\'m now diving into the precise placement of elements. I\'m focusing on the component\'s architecture, including distinct pain points and solution points. I\'m leveraging the data array to set up the path based on pain and solution states to set up the component structure in `OrganicJourney.tsx`. I will adjust the layout to create the desktop view. I will integrate the `TrendingUp` icon.\n\n\n**Adjusting the Visual Strategy**\n\nI\'m now iterating on the SVG path design. A simple, vertical line with slight organic curvature, animated to \"light up\" sequentially, feels like the best approach. I\'ll make the line a sine wave, or bezier, and render the elements inside the svg with explicit placement. My focus is now on ensuring it animates cleanly.\n\n\n**Refining the Golden Thread**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the SVG path and its visual execution. After experimenting with `preserveAspectRatio` and finding it less than ideal, I am now focused on using `vectorEffect=\"non-scaling-stroke\"` to keep the curves clean and beautiful. I plan to construct the component in `OrganicJourney.tsx` to handle this. I am now creating a beautiful curving golden thread to address the visual goal.\n\n\n**Animating the Visual Journey**\n\nI\'m now implementing the SVG path animation using GSAP. I\'m focusing on the curving golden thread and the overall flow. I\'ve set the path\'s initial `stroke-dasharray` and offset, and I\'m using `ScrollTrigger` to scrub the animation based on scroll progress within the container. I am also working on adding animations for the individual journey cards using GSAP\'s `utils.toArray`.\n\n\n\n**Implementing Dot Animation**\n\nI\'m now implementing the dot animation, using GSAP to scale and fade in each dot on scroll trigger. I\'m focusing on the animation of the journey cards and syncing them with the animated path, which will create a harmonious visual flow. I\'m working on the initial layout and plan to update the UI visibility as I continue to synchronize.\n\n\n**Orchestrating the Animation**\n\nI\'m now focusing on orchestrating the dot and card animations with the SVG path\'s progress. I\'m leveraging GSAP\'s `ScrollTrigger` to synchronize each element with the animated path, using an array of journey steps. I\'ve begun to construct the visual layout in `OrganicJourney.tsx`.\n\n\n\n**Refining the UI Animation**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the user interface animation. I\'m focusing on the interaction between the SVG path animation, the golden thread, and the text. I\'m aiming to keep the curve simple. I\'m using an overlay with `vectorEffect=\"non-scaling-stroke\"` to address previous scaling issues and will use the `OrganicJourney.tsx` file to construct the golden thread.\n\n\n\n**Synchronizing the UI**\n\nI\'m now synchronizing the UI elements with the SVG path\'s progress. I\'m leveraging GSAP and scroll trigger. Using the golden thread, the animations of the text and images will create a smooth interaction. I\'m using an overlay with `vectorEffect=\"non-scaling-stroke\"` to fix scaling issues. I am now working on the UI and SVG alignment in `OrganicJourney.tsx`.\n\n\n\n**Aligning the Visual Components**\n\nI\'m now integrating the visual components, using the journey cards with their content. I\'m leveraging Tailwind classes to manage flexbox layouts and adjust component padding for the card\'s visual content. I will align the SVG path with these elements to create a more consistent visual hierarchy. I am focused on the layout strategy of the elements.\n\n\n**Adjusting the UI Alignment**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the finer details, particularly the alignment between the golden thread and the journey cards\' content, especially on desktop. I\'m focusing on the center dot, which is only visible on desktop, and its placement and animation. I\'m taking the height disparity in the layout into account and adjusting with flexbox classes to align elements.\n\n\n**Revising the Path Strategy**\n\nI\'m now revising my approach to the path design. Using a straight vertical SVG line, `M 50 0 L 50 100`, combined with CSS for organic styling, is the new focus. Bezier curves, while visually appealing, introduce complexity for even distribution across steps, given the S-curve. This allows for a more responsive and less math-intensive solution. I plan to use CSS `justify-content: space-between` to distribute the steps.\n\n\n**Refining the Organic Curve**\n\nI\'m now implementing the organic curve as an SVG path. I\'ve opted for a sine wave-like path defined by a quadratic Bezier curve and subsequent smooth curves, which I\'m testing mentally. The goal is to perfectly center the curve in the layout and smoothly connect the journey stages, by drawing the path through the center (X=50), and letting it snake around the center.\n\n\n"
